WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.594
Unauthorized-access hunts examine whether people or systems reached OT assets outside approved identity,

00:00:05.594 --> 00:00:07.285
path, time, or privilege.

00:00:07.285 --> 00:00:12.357
Start with the authorized-access model: which vendors, employees, service accounts, jump hosts,

00:00:12.357 --> 00:00:15.315
VPNs, destinations, and maintenance windows are expected.

00:00:15.315 --> 00:00:20.387
Review remote-access and VPN sessions for unusual source systems, locations, login times,

00:00:20.387 --> 00:00:21.655
duration, and targets.

00:00:21.655 --> 00:00:25.882
Correlate jump-host activity with identity events, ticket approval, firewall connections,

00:00:25.882 --> 00:00:27.149
and destination logs.

00:00:27.149 --> 00:00:31.799
A successful session without a corresponding work record may require validation.

00:00:31.799 --> 00:00:33.489
Shared accounts complicate attribution.

00:00:33.489 --> 00:00:36.025
Hunters should identify where they remain,

00:00:36.025 --> 00:00:39.829
whether use can be tied to an individual session,

00:00:39.829 --> 00:00:42.365
and whether privilege exceeds the task.

00:00:42.365 --> 00:00:47.859
Service accounts deserve separate baselines because interactive or cross-zone use may be unexpected.

00:00:47.859 --> 00:00:53.354
Look for access that bypasses managed entry points, originates from an unapproved workstation,

00:00:53.354 --> 00:00:57.580
reaches an unusual asset, or continues beyond the authorized window.

00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:02.652
Failed logons, privilege changes, and access after account expiration can add context,

00:01:02.652 --> 00:01:05.188
but none proves intent by itself.

00:01:05.188 --> 00:01:08.569
Coordinate findings with operations and the access owner.

00:01:08.569 --> 00:01:12.796
Emergency support, outages, or field work may explain unusual timing.

00:01:12.796 --> 00:01:16.177
Preserve session and approval evidence before it expires,

00:01:16.177 --> 00:01:19.981
and avoid terminating active access without understanding process consequence.

00:01:19.981 --> 00:01:24.630
The hunt should conclude with affected accounts and assets, evidence, confidence,

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:25.898
and recommended follow-up.

00:01:25.898 --> 00:01:30.124
Findings may improve access policy, account ownership, monitoring, vendor procedures,

00:01:30.124 --> 00:01:32.660
and detections for future unauthorized pathways.
